<article class="env-lemma" id="062S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/062S">Lemma <span data-tag="062S">29.32.5</span>.</a> Let $Z \to Y \to X$ be immersions of schemes. Then there is a canonical exact sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  i^*\mathcal{C}_{Y/X} \to \mathcal{C}_{Z/X} \to \mathcal{C}_{Z/Y} \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> where the maps come from Lemma <a href="/tag/01R4" data-tag="01R4">29.32.3</a> and $i : Z \to Y$ is the first morphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Via Lemma <a href="/tag/01R4" data-tag="01R4">29.32.3</a> this translates into the following algebra fact. Suppose that $C \to B \to A$ are surjective ring maps. Let $I = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(B \to A)$, $J = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(C \to A)$ and $K = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(C \to B)$. Then there is an exact sequence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  K/K^2 \otimes _ B A \to J/J^2 \to I/I^2 \to 0.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       This follows immediately from the observation that $I = J/K$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>